eyword]])\n? --\n\n+         LogicalTableScan(table=[[movie_keyword]])\n-         LogicalTableScan(table=[[title]])\n? --\n\n+       LogicalTableScan(table=[[title]])\n  \n```"}], 'response': "Step 1: The query rewrite rule applies to the given SQL query by identifying the relational expression changes made by the rule.\n\nStep 2: Parsing the logical plan changes into detailed changes of the given SQL query, we can see that the original query has been rewritten with a new LogicalAggregate operator and some joins have been added. The `SELECT` clause now contains an aggregate function `MIN(t.title)` applied to the `title` column. The `FROM` clause remains the same.\n\nStep 3: Since the query rewrite rule contains multiple cases, we need to identify which case is matched during the query rewrite. In this case, it seems that Case 1 of the query rewrite rule applies because the SQL query contains a `GROUP BY` clause (although it's not explicitly shown in the logical plan changes) and the aggregate functions operate directly on columns.\n\nStep 4: The query rewrite process can be explained as follows:\n\nThe original query is rewritten by applying the `MIN(t.title)` aggregate function to the data source referred to in the `FROM` clause of the inner query before the projection. This means that the `title` column is grouped and then the minimum title is selected for each group.\n\nThe logical plan changes show that a new LogicalAggregate operator has been added, which applies the `MIN(t.title)` function to the data. The original `SELECT` clause with the `MIN(t.title)` aggregate function remains unchanged.\n\nAdditionally, some joins have been added to the rewritten query to combine the necessary tables (`keyword`, `movie_info`, `movie_keyword`, and `title`). These joins are used to filter the data based on certain conditions specified in the `WHERE` clause.", 'time': 9.144424676895142}
